        {"type": "prose", "id": "intro", "payload": {"text": "A circuit breaker is like an electrical fuse: when too many failures occur, it 'trips' and stops current from flowing. In software, this means: stop calling a failing service so it can recover, and so your service doesn't waste resources waiting for timeouts.\n\nThe pattern has three states:\n\n- **Closed** (normal): requests flow through. Failures are counted.\n- **Open** (tripped): requests fail immediately — no call is made to the downstream. This gives the downstream time to recover.\n- **Half-Open** (testing): after a cooldown, a few requests are allowed through. If they succeed, the breaker closes. If they fail, it opens again."}},
