        {"type": "prose", "id": "isolation", "payload": {"text": "Bulkheads can be implemented at different levels:\n\n- **Thread pools**: separate pool per downstream service. Most common.\n- **Connection pools**: separate HTTP/TCP connection pool per downstream.\n- **Processes**: separate processes (or containers) per service type. Strongest isolation.\n- **Machines**: separate servers per workload. Physical isolation.\n\nThe stronger the isolation, the more resource-expensive. Thread pools are the sweet spot for most cases."}},
        {"type": "callout", "id": "combine", "payload": {"title": "Bulkhead + Circuit Breaker", "body": "Bulkheads limit the blast radius. Circuit breakers stop calling a failing service entirely. Together: the circuit breaker prevents new calls to a failing service, and the bulkhead ensures that even if the breaker is slow to trip, other services aren't affected. This combination is the foundation of resilient microservice architecture.", "kind": "success"}},
        {"type": "quiz", "id": "q1", "payload": {"question": "Your service has a single thread pool of 200 threads and calls 3 downstream services. One downstream starts responding slowly. What happens without a bulkhead?", "shape": "mcq", "options": ["Only requests to the slow downstream are affected.", "The slow downstream consumes all 200 threads, and requests to the other two downstreams also fail.", "The service automatically scales up more threads.", "Nothing — 200 threads is enough for anyone."], "answer_index": 1, "rationale": "Without a bulkhead, all three downstreams share the same 200-thread pool. The slow downstream causes each of its requests to hold a thread for longer (waiting for the slow response). As more requests come in, all 200 threads eventually get consumed by the slow downstream, leaving no threads for the other two — they fail too, even though they're healthy. This is why bulkheads (separate thread pools per downstream) are essential in resilient architecture.", "difficulty": "interview"}},
